%% Outbound effect lifecycle — the claim/commit/reconcile protocol.
%% This is the mechanism behind edge cases 7, 8, 11 and 14.
stateDiagram-v2
    direction TB

    [*] --> Deriving

    Deriving: Derive key from stable inputs only
    note right of Deriving
      v1:{domain}:{entity}:{id}:{occurrence}
      Never $execution.id, never a timestamp,
      never an attempt counter. Purity here is
      what makes replay (case 14) free.
    end note

    Deriving --> Claiming

    Claiming: INSERT ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING RETURNING
    Claiming --> Owned: row returned — we own the effect
    Claiming --> Observed: zero rows — someone got here first

    Owned --> Calling
    Calling: external provider call

    Calling --> Succeeded: 2xx
    Calling --> Retryable: 429 / 408 / 5xx / timeout
    Calling --> Terminal: other 4xx / missing credentials
    Calling --> Ambiguous: process died before settling

    Retryable --> Backoff
    Backoff: requeue at random(0, min(2s*2^n, 15m))
    Backoff --> Claiming: attempt < 5
    Backoff --> Terminal: attempts exhausted

    Observed --> Succeeded: stored state = succeeded, reuse response
    Observed --> Terminal: stored state = failed_permanent
    Observed --> Backoff: in_flight and fresh — another worker owns it
    Observed --> Ambiguous: in_flight and stale

    Ambiguous: Did the effect land? Lost ack — case 7
    Ambiguous --> Reconciling
    Reconciling: ask the provider — lookup by idempotency key
    Reconciling --> Succeeded: found, adopt provider_ref
    Reconciling --> Backoff: not found, safe to retry

    Terminal --> DeadLetter
    DeadLetter: dead_letter row keeps the original key
    DeadLetter --> Claiming: operator replay re-derives the SAME key

    Succeeded --> [*]

    note left of Succeeded
      Idempotent by construction: every later
      attempt lands in Observed and short-circuits.
      Case 8 (WhatsApp retry) proven externally by
      asserting the mock journal saw exactly one send.
    end note
